From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 12:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] AMLUtils: add shared display timing helpers

Three things are about to want to know where the display is in its frame and how
many refreshes a frame of content is shown for: the latency measurement, the frame
scheduling and the phase alignment. Put the reads somewhere they can share before
any of them is written, rather than letting three copies grow and disagree.

Whether the display is a whole multiple of the content rate is not decided
again here. CRenderManager::CheckEnableClockSync() already decides it, and a
second comparison on another copy of the frame rate disagrees with it exactly
where it matters: a 23.976 mode divided in float lands a rounding error off the
content rate, and a strict test there disqualified every 23.976 title. So the
renderer is offered the clock sync together with that verdict - the display
refreshes per frame CRenderManager found matching, TakeClockSync() - and the
Amlogic renderer records it as a whole number. Content faster than the display
(48fps on 24Hz: Kodi counts it a match) records zero, since frames are dropped
there rather than each shown a whole number of times. Here the renderer still
hands the clock sync back; the alignment that takes it over comes later in the
series.

Shared code by necessity: the verdict exists only inside CRenderManager, so the
renderer interface gains one default-false call and CheckEnableClockSync() one
line to make it.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
